WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.032 --> 00:00:04.848
An old saying goes: "One man's meat is another man's poison."

2
00:00:04.849 --> 00:00:10.736
In much the same way, nuclear technology brings to mind different things for different people.

3
00:00:10.737 --> 00:00:15.896
To some, it is a useful tool in medicine and an efficient source of energy.

4
00:00:15.897 --> 00:00:21.043
To others, it conjures up images of mushroom clouds and mass destruction.

5
00:00:21.044 --> 00:00:26.228
Understanding nuclear technology begins with the nucleus, the heart of the atom.

6
00:00:26.229 --> 00:00:34.157
All matter is made up of atoms, and every atom consists of particles called neutrons, protons, and electrons.

7
00:00:34.158 --> 00:00:43.375
Neutrons and protons form the solid nucleus of the atom, while the much smaller electrons orbit the nucleus like the planets around the sun.

8
00:00:43.376 --> 00:00:49.309
The number of these three particles in an atom determines what kind of matter, or element, it is.

9
00:00:49.310 --> 00:00:55.815
For instance, carbon has six protons, six neutrons, and six electrons.

10
00:00:55.816 --> 00:01:00.908
The ratio of these particles within an atom determines whether the atom is stable.

11
00:01:00.909 --> 00:01:10.678
Nature does not like instability, so unstable atoms undergo a natural process called "radioactive decay," in which they release extra particles.

12
00:01:10.679 --> 00:01:20.069
Radioactive decay can change an atom from one element into another element or into another form of the same element, which is known as an isotope.

13
00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:24.730
Isotopes occur when the number of neutrons present in the nucleus differs.

14
00:01:24.731 --> 00:01:30.714
Carbon-14, for example, is an isotope of carbon with eight neutrons instead of six.

15
00:01:30.715 --> 00:01:37.058
The particles released by radioactive decay can be harmful, but they can be put to good use as well.

16
00:01:37.059 --> 00:01:41.610
In the field of nuclear medicine, radiation is used to treat cancer.

17
00:01:41.611 --> 00:01:50.102
Radioactive elements can also be injected into the body and tracked with imaging equipment to provide a look inside the body without surgery.

18
00:01:50.103 --> 00:01:54.035
This procedure is very useful in medical diagnosis.

19
00:01:54.036 --> 00:02:02.695
When the United States' Food and Drug Administration tests new drugs for safety, many of the tests are conducted using nuclear processes.

20
00:02:02.696 --> 00:02:07.200
Carbon dating is another technique that relies on nuclear processes.

21
00:02:07.201 --> 00:02:14.469
In the field of archaeology, it is used to determine the age of artifacts based on the radioactive decay of carbon-14.

22
00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:26.569
Because the carbon-14 isotope decays into carbon-12 at a very slow and constant rate, you can determine the age of an object by measuring the amount of carbon-14 it contains.

23
00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:35.507
Museums also use carbon dating to verify the age of pieces of art. Nuclear technology is present even in our everyday lives.

24
00:02:35.508 --> 00:02:41.768
Many smoke detectors, for example, employ a radioactive material to trigger the alarm.

25
00:02:41.769 --> 00:02:46.884
In addition to releasing particles, radioactive decay also releases energy.

26
00:02:46.885 --> 00:02:54.503
Uranium, a much heavier radioactive element than carbon-14, is the one most often used to produce nuclear power.

27
00:02:54.504 --> 00:03:02.805
One of its isotopes, uranium-235 (U-235), can be induced to split in a process called nuclear fission.

28
00:03:02.806 --> 00:03:13.325
If a free neutron hits a U-235 nucleus, the atom will absorb the neutron but will become unstable. It will then split in order to stabilize itself.

29
00:03:13.326 --> 00:03:22.426
Each split releases several neutrons, which cause more fission reactions, in turn releasing more neutrons—igniting a chain reaction.

30
00:03:22.427 --> 00:03:25.458
This process releases great amounts of energy.

31
00:03:25.459 --> 00:03:29.530
Nuclear power plants use this energy to produce electricity.

32
00:03:29.531 --> 00:03:37.610
The energy of the chain reaction is used to create steam to turn a turbine, just as in other power stations.

33
00:03:37.611 --> 00:03:44.666
One pound of uranium, however, is able to produce 3.7 million times as much energy as a pound of coal.

34
00:03:44.667 --> 00:03:54.209
Plutonium, an element that is formed in uranium-based nuclear reactions, can also be used for fission, making nuclear energy very efficient.

35
00:03:54.210 --> 00:04:02.434
Nuclear power accounts for 17 percent of the world's electricity, and many people see it as a viable alternative to fossil fuels.

36
00:04:02.435 --> 00:04:08.321
Our earth has only a limited amount of coal and oil left, but uranium is abundant.

37
00:04:08.322 --> 00:04:14.535
In addition, a well-designed and maintained nuclear power plant produces clean electricity.

38
00:04:14.536 --> 00:04:23.415
In fact, a coal-fired plant may disperse more radioactive substances into the atmosphere than a properly functioning nuclear facility.

39
00:04:23.416 --> 00:04:33.247
Plants that burn fossil fuels also eject tons of other pollutants, such as carbon dioxide, sulfur, and ash, into the atmosphere each year.

40
00:04:33.248 --> 00:04:36.905
Nuclear stations do not release these pollutants.

41
00:04:36.906 --> 00:04:40.577
There are, however, disadvantages to nuclear power.

42
00:04:40.578 --> 00:04:47.948
Exposure to radioactive particles can cause radiation sickness and lead to cancer or other health problems.

43
00:04:47.949 --> 00:04:52.391
Many precautions are taken to ensure that nuclear reactors are safe.

44
00:04:52.392 --> 00:05:01.663
Since particles released by radioactive decay cannot penetrate thick concrete, reactors are encased in concrete buildings that act as shields.

45
00:05:01.664 --> 00:05:09.178
However, despite the numerous safety measures taken at nuclear power plants, history has shown that accidents happen.

46
00:05:09.179 --> 00:05:18.705
In 1979, radiation leaked from the Three-Mile Island Nuclear Generating Station in the U.S., and thousands of people had to be evacuated.

47
00:05:18.706 --> 00:05:20.503
Luckily, no one died.

48
00:05:20.504 --> 00:05:35.645
In 1986, an accidental explosion at the Chernobyl Nuclear Power Station in the former Soviet Union was not contained, and thousands of civilians and military personnel were exposed to radiation.

49
00:05:35.646 --> 00:05:47.848
Thirty people died from the explosion or soon after, and within the next several years, thousands more experienced a variety of health problems due to the radiation they received.

50
00:05:47.849 --> 00:05:54.800
In recent years, many people have also become concerned that nuclear reactors are vulnerable to attack.

51
00:05:54.801 --> 00:06:02.951
A bomb or sabotage at a nuclear facility could cause a horrendous disaster, as the accident at Chernobyl has shown.

52
00:06:02.952 --> 00:06:12.667
Not surprisingly, the risks associated with nuclear power have given rise to the expression "NIMBY," an acronym for "not in my backyard."

53
00:06:12.668 --> 00:06:16.222
This phrase captures the sentiments of much of the public.

54
00:06:16.223 --> 00:06:24.475
Although people appreciate the electricity a nuclear reactor provides, they do not want a potential radioactive "bomb" near their homes.

55
00:06:24.476 --> 00:06:29.309
The NIMBY sentiment applies even more to the disposal of nuclear waste.

56
00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:35.643
Waste products from nuclear reactors are highly radioactive and cannot be simply thrown away.

57
00:06:35.644 --> 00:06:44.526
Even though containers for nuclear waste are designed to stop radioactive leaks, the material inside can remain toxic for thousands of years.

58
00:06:44.527 --> 00:06:49.796
Even the strongest containers will deteriorate and fail before the material is safe.

59
00:06:49.797 --> 00:06:59.336
Used fuel rods from nuclear reactors are cooled in tightly sealed containers in pools of water for several years and then buried deep underground.

60
00:06:59.337 --> 00:07:05.953
There is no guarantee, however, that measures like these will be enough to protect people and the environment.

61
00:07:05.954 --> 00:07:12.890
Nuclear technology poses another less direct, but equally important risk—nuclear proliferation.

62
00:07:12.891 --> 00:07:25.551
Although nuclear weapons and nuclear power plants require different grades of uranium, the technology used to produce nuclear power can also be used to develop weapons capable of killing millions of people.

63
00:07:25.552 --> 00:07:34.550
The two superpowers of the cold war, the U.S. and Russia, have enough nuclear bombs to kill every person on earth many times over.

64
00:07:34.551 --> 00:07:42.757
As more nations acquire nuclear power and possibly nuclear weapons, the likelihood of a devastating nuclear war increases.

65
00:07:42.758 --> 00:07:49.567
Clearly, nuclear technology has advantages and disadvantages, and there is a heated debate about its use.

66
00:07:49.568 --> 00:07:58.375
As the countries of the world plan for a cleaner, safer, and more energy-efficient future, they must weigh the pros and cons of this technology carefully.

